{
  "id": "80677",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Nuclear Structure and the Strong Nuclear Force",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Nuclear chemistry"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The study of the structure of atomic nuclei and the processes that cause changes in nuclear structure."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "552820097",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "633902872",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the study of reactions that involve changes in nuclear structure. Earlier modules on atoms, molecules, and ions introduced the basic idea of nuclear structure: that the nucleus of an atom is composed of protons and, except for "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_1^1\\mathrm H",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", neutrons. Recall that the number of protons in the nucleus is called the atomic number (Z) of the element, and the sum of the number of protons and the number of neutrons is the mass number (A). Atoms with the same atomic number but different mass numbers are isotopes of the same element. When referring to a single type of nucleus, we often use the term "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "nuclide"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The nucleus of a particular isotope, characterized by a specific number of protons and neutrons."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "972947748",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1914049935",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " and identify it by the notation "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_{\\mathrm Z}^{\\mathrm A}\\mathrm X",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", where X is the symbol for the element, A is the mass number, and Z is the atomic number (for example, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": "). A nuclide is often referenced by the element's name followed by a hyphen and the mass number. For example, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_6^{14}\\mathrm C",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " is called “carbon-14.” The images below show different ways in which isotopes are represented, as well as the three isotopes of magnesium."
              }
            ],
            "id": "hm5ectxjvmr4z5e",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "Three different ways to represent an isotope of magnesium-24.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ytu5yjtc3w0rwzm",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "wxbmed1iwa3d4kp",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/c0/c0cd4bb4f8a90426fe302ed0fd1e2cc6/mag_24_reps.PNG",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 700
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "760094906",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "Symbols for 3 isotopes of magnesium. The one on the left has a mass number of 24 and an atomic number of 12 and indicates that it has 12 protons and 12 neutrons. The one in the center has a mass number of 25 and an atomic number of 12 and indicates that it has 12 protons and 13 neutrons. The one on the right has a mass number of 26 and an atomic number of 12 and indicates that it has 12 protons and 14 neutrons.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "cccoq4yxca7b47k",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "mzv10mvf5l7s26m",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/4c/4cbb605016082387c5287ec90f345b07/Mg_isotopes.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 700
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Protons and neutrons, collectively called nucleons"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A collective term for the protons and neutrons within a nucleus."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1024117329",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3241480566",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", are packed together tightly in a nucleus. With a radius of about 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−15"
              },
              {
                "text": " meters, a nucleus is very small compared to the radius of the entire atom, which is about 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−10"
              },
              {
                "text": " meters. Nuclei are extremely dense compared to bulk matter, averaging 1.8 × 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "14"
              },
              {
                "text": " grams per cubic centimeter. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "lxi6uuiokwmfsti",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2296006917",
