      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "8x0gxgo43malh1s",
                "text": "Strong Nuclear Force"
              }
            ],
            "id": "mbnws1lhn1gkpt6",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "To hold positively charged protons together in the very small volume of a nucleus requires very strong attractive forces because the positively charged protons repel one another strongly at such short distances. The force of attraction that holds the nucleus together is the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "strong nuclear force"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The force of attraction between nucleons (protons and neutrons) that holds the nucleus together, overcoming the repulsive force between positively charged protons."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "864071721",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2829861337",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". (The strong force is one of the four fundamental forces that are known to exist. The others are the electromagnetic force, the gravitational force, and the nuclear weak force.) This force acts between protons, between neutrons, and between protons and neutrons. It differs greatly from the electrostatic force that holds negatively charged electrons around a positively charged nucleus (the attraction between opposite charges). Over distances less than 10"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−15"
              },
              {
                "text": " meters and within the nucleus, the strong nuclear force is much stronger than electrostatic repulsions between protons; over larger distances and outside the nucleus, it is essentially nonexistent."
              }
            ],
            "id": "s6qbsgh8vxj69pf",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
